# TEL-P2-026 — application role least privilege, measured on production
# Instance: telestar-db (telestar-crm-final, asia-southeast1)
# Database: telestar_crm   Role: crm
# Executed from inside the running production web container crm-4-u-web-1,
# reached over an IAP tunnel to telestar-crm-vm. Operator authorized the write.
# Date: 2026-08-27

--- 1. privileges before (measured, not asserted) ---
$ SELECT rolname, rolcreatedb, rolcreaterole, rolsuper, rolbypassrls
    FROM pg_roles WHERE rolname = current_user;
[{"rolname":"crm","rolcreatedb":true,"rolcreaterole":true,"rolsuper":false,"rolbypassrls":false}]

--- 2. the change ---
$ ALTER ROLE crm NOCREATEDB NOCREATEROLE;

--- 3. privileges after (read back from pg_roles, same session) ---
[{"rolname":"crm","rolcreatedb":false,"rolcreaterole":false,"rolsuper":false,"rolbypassrls":false}]

--- 4. migration path still works without the revoked privileges ---
#
# This is the check the defect required: a deploy that had ever relied on CREATEDB or
# CREATEROLE would begin failing at the next release rather than at the moment of the
# change, so the migration path is exercised immediately rather than assumed.
#
$ prisma migrate status
Datasource "db": PostgreSQL database "telestar_crm", schema "public" at "136.110.29.201:5432"
54 migrations found in prisma/migrations
Database schema is up to date!

$ prisma migrate deploy
54 migrations found in prisma/migrations
No pending migrations to apply.

--- 5. the application still reads through the same role ---
$ prisma.user.count()
users: 44

--- 6. production health after the change, three consecutive probes ---
$ curl -s https://crm.telestar.cloud/api/health
http=200 {"ok":true,"commit":"c63673d1fb33b7fa8ed9e21b66711d28998fd53a","schema":"ready"}
http=200 {"ok":true,"commit":"c63673d1fb33b7fa8ed9e21b66711d28998fd53a","schema":"ready"}
http=200 {"ok":true,"commit":"c63673d1fb33b7fa8ed9e21b66711d28998fd53a","schema":"ready"}

--- reversal path ---
# ALTER ROLE crm CREATEDB CREATEROLE;
# Recovery if the role could no longer alter itself: Cloud SQL retains an administrative
# path through `gcloud sql users set-password postgres`, so the change is not one-way.

--- what this log does NOT prove ---
# It does not prove any source file contains the words NOCREATEDB or NOCREATEROLE.
# tests/db-role-least-privilege.test.ts asserts that provisioning scripts declare those
# attributes, which keeps a future re-provision from reintroducing the privileges. That is
# a source-text property and is not evidence about the live instance. The live instance is
# evidenced by sections 1-3 above and by nothing else.
